    <solution><m>\small{g(x) = \ln(x)}</m> is <m>\small{(0,\infty)}</m>, and that the graph
contains the point (1,0). The range is <m>\small{(-\infty,\infty)}</m>.




(click on graph to enlarge)



<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/sec-exp-log-8-13-image-5.png" width="33%"/>

The graph of <m>\small{f(x) = 5+\ln\mathopen{}\left(x-5\right)}</m> represents a transformation of 5 units to the right and 5 units
upward. This will not affect the range, but the domain will become <m>\small{\left(5,\infty \right)}</m>. The point
(1,0) on the graph of <m>\small{g(x) = \ln(x)}</m> will correspond to the point (6,5) on the graph
of <m>\small{f(x)}</m>, and the vertical asymptote at the <m>\small{y}</m> axis will become the vertical
line <m>\small{x = 5}</m>. This is shown by the graph at (d).





</solution>
